WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.440
- All right y'all, before we go

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.520
I have one last story
that might make you smile,

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:04.543
this is "What I'm Liking."

00:00:04.543 --> 00:00:07.870
(upbeat music)

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.170
Earlier, we talked about
our love for books,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.710
something many of us share,

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.010
that also includes a
Utah woman who went viral

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:16.350
for creating mini books, check this out.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.920
- Was it absolutely necessary

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:19.960
to make tiny replicas of the books

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.210
that I've read over the last year

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.250
just so that I could put them in a jar

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.680
and have a visual representation
of my own accomplishments

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.400
for my own validation?

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.710
No, no, it was not necessary.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:32.040
Do I regret spending two and a half hours

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.323
of my night last night making them?

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.370
Also no, they're my favorite thing

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.203
and I regret nothing.

00:00:38.203 --> 00:00:39.793
- Oh my God
(audience clapping)

00:00:39.793 --> 00:00:43.010
She needs a show, she's so funny.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.710
They look adorable and
after the video went viral

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.690
her inbox was flooded with messages

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:48.700
asking how she made them

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.230
and probably just as many
saying how funny she is.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.970
We have Stevie on the line right now

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.607
who watches on KSL-TV in Salt Lake City.

00:00:55.607 --> 00:00:56.918
(audience cheering)

00:00:56.918 --> 00:00:57.783
What's up Stevie?

00:00:58.677 --> 00:00:59.940
- Hi.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.700
- You, I have to lead with, yes

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.660
this is such a cool idea,
but you are so funny.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:07.010
Your comedic timing is on point ma'am.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:08.321
- Thank you.

00:01:08.321 --> 00:01:11.100
- So, where did you get the idea from?

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.160
- Well, you know, I'd seen a similar trend

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.720
with Christmas ornaments

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.730
and people were tracking
the books that they had read

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.160
like in a Christmas ornament.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.540
And it was, it inspired me

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.150
'cause I have this kind of silly problem

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.580
where, like only half
of the books that I read

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.720
are like the physical book

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.150
and the rest are digital or audible

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.160
and I know that that's a hot take

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:34.330
and that some people are gonna say

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.170
that doesn't count as reading,

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.350
but it is my jar and it counts 'cause

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:38.930
I'm the one reading them.
(host laughing)

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.350
But this was like,

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.070
this was the perfect
solution for that, right?

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:44.730
Like I could have of my audio books,

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.570
my digital books, and
nobody knows the difference.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.580
So that's where I kind of got the idea.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.480
But I played around with the design.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.810
I figured out how I wanted to make them.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:58.810
And then I spent a night and
a bottle of wine making them.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.650
I made a little over 20 of them,

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.060
I think.
- [Host] Yeah.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.870
- And I've added to it since then.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:09.360
In fact we have a new addition to the jar.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.770
- [Host] Yes, "Pieces Of Her."
- It's Karen Slaughter's

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:11.623
"Pieces Of Her."
(audience clapping)

00:02:11.623 --> 00:02:14.140
It's a Netflix series now.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.410
Highly recommend, fantastic book.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.243
- I love that.

00:02:17.243 --> 00:02:18.790
I actually love you read the "Dutch House"

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:19.623
and "The Midnight Library."

00:02:19.623 --> 00:02:20.810
I love those two, I read those as well.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.770
I was trying to, I was
peeking at all your books

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:23.690
while I was looking.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:25.310
Thank you so much, Stevie.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.571
I'm liking your post right
now, but I'm loving you.

00:02:27.571 --> 00:02:30.100
(audience clapping)

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.130
That's not all, our season-long partner,

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.660
Pilot Pen Makers Of G2,

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.160
The Go To Pen Of The Kelly Clarkson Show

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.010
wanna honor ultimate
overachievers, just like you ma'am,

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:39.070
so they're gonna be writing you a check

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:41.625
for a thousand dollars just
'cause we think you're cool.

00:02:41.625 --> 00:02:42.458
(host chuckles)

00:02:42.458 --> 00:02:44.327
- Oh my God.
(audience cheering)

00:02:44.327 --> 00:02:46.177
- All right, thank you so much Stevie,

00:02:46.177 --> 00:02:47.172
have a good day.

